    "lawCode": "Amd §§3000-a & 3000-b, Pub Health L",
    "lawSection": "Public Health Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S7603\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\nAn act to amend the public health law, in relation to causes of action\nagainst certain businesses arising in connection with the AED\n\n\n PURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL :\nThis bill would remove barriers to AED use by allowing persons to\noperate an AED other than as part of their employment, who act in good\nfaith and with reasonable care, and without expectation of monetary\ncompensation, even if they were not trained in the use of an AED.\n\n SUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS :\nSection one amends subdivision two of section 3000-a of the public\nhealth law by expanding the scope of the Good Samaritan law to include\nany person who or entity or group that operates and/or facilitates\nimplementation or makes available resuscitation equipment that\nfacilitates first aid, including an AED,\n\nSection two amends subdivision three-of section 3000-b of the public\nhealth law by providing that a person who operates an AED other than\nas part of or incidental to his employment or regular duties, who is\nacting in good faith, with reasonable care, and without expectation of\nmonetary compensation, may provide first aid that includes operation\nof an AED without having completed training. It further provides that\nthe section shall not limit any Good Samaritan protections provided\nin, section 3000-a of the public health law,\n\nSection three provides the effective date.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\nSudden cardiac arrest is one of the leading causes of death in the\nUnited States, especially among those 40 years of. age and older. (1)\nAccording to the American Heart Association, nearly 250,000\nout-of-hospital deaths occur annually. as a result of sudden cardiac\narrest.(2) Because so many incidents of sudden cardiac arrest occur\noutside of a hospital, with over two thirds of the episodes occurring\nin homes(3), a medical professional or a person trained to administer\nemergency first including but not limited to CPR and/or use of an AED,\nis rarely the first person on the scene. Thus, lay rescuers often have\nthe important and time-sensitive task of administering primary urgent\ncare in the form of CPR and/or use of an AED.\n\nGood Sam protections within the public health law guarantee that any\nperson who voluntarily and without expectation of a monetary reward\nprovides urgently needed medical attention will not be liable for\ndamages for harm alleged to have occurred, unless it is shown that he\nor she is guilty of negligence or intentional, misconduct.  Currently,\nGood Samaritan protections extend only to those individuals who or\ngroups that purchase or make available resuscitation equipment such as\nan AED, omitting the entire population who might operate and/or\nfacilitate operation of an AED. Furthermore, Good Samaritan\nprotections only apply to AED lay rescuers who have undergone a\ntraining course in how to use a defibrillator. This legislation aims\nto clarify that any person who or any entity or group that legally\npurchases, operates, and/or facilitates operation of an AED is\nprotected under current Good Samaritan provisions in the public health\nlaw, except where the person is guilty of negligence, gross negligence\nor intentional misconduct.\n\nThe American Heart Association has determined that concerns over\nliability are a huge barrier to AED usage. However, modern AEDs are\n\"highly accurate, user-friendly computerized devices.\"(4)  Given the\nsimplicity of the devices, and the fact that an AED will not\nadminister shock to someone who does not need it, this legislation\nwill encourage AED use in the event of an emergency. Action by a\nperson trained to properly use an AED is always preferable, and it is\nimportant that persons expected to operate an AED as part of or\nincidental to his employment or regular duties should be trained in\nthe use of an. AED. However, when that option is not viable, it is\nimportant to promote the administration of emergency first aid over\ninaction. In the event of sudden cardiac arrest, fast action is often\nthe difference between life and death.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\nThis is a new bill.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATION :\nNone to the state.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n FOOTNOTES :\n(1) Zheng Z3, Croft 113, Giles WH, Mensah GA, \"Sudden cardiac death in\nthe United States, 1989 to 1998.\" Circulation, 2001; 104: 2158-2163.\n(2) Heart Disease and Stroke statistics-2005 Update. Dallas, Texas:\nAmerican Heart Association; 2005.\n(3) Aufderheide, Tom, et al. \"Community Lay Rescuer Automated External\nDefibrillation Programs Key State Legislative Components and\nImplementation Strategies. Circulation: Journal of the American Heart\nAssociation, 28 Feb 2006; 8.\n(4) Aufderheide, Tom, et al, \"Community Lay Rescuer Automated External\nDefibrillation Programs Key State Legislative Components and\nImplementation Strategies.\" Circulation: Journal of the American Heart\nAssociation. 28 Feb 2006; 2.\n",
